### What are setFirstResult and setMaxResults?

These are the raw JPA methods on the Query interface for pagination. setFirstResult sets the zero-based row offset, and setMaxResults sets the page size. They translate directly to SQL LIMIT and OFFSET.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class RawPaginationService {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<Transaction> getPage(
            String accountId, int page, int size) {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT t FROM Transaction t "
          + "WHERE t.accountId = :acct "
          + "ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC, t.id DESC",
            Transaction.class
        ).setParameter("acct", accountId)
         .setFirstResult(page * size)  // offset
         .setMaxResults(size)           // limit
         .getResultList();
    }
}
```

```text
Page 0, size 20:
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0

Page 2, size 20:
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20 OFFSET 40
  (Returns rows 41-60)
```

### How does Spring Data Pageable work?

Spring Data's Pageable interface bundles the page number, page size, and sort direction into one object. You pass it to a repository method, and Spring Data generates the appropriate LIMIT/OFFSET and ORDER BY clauses.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Slice;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

public interface TransactionRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Transaction, Long> {

    // Returns Page (with total count)
    Page<Transaction> findByAccountId(String accountId, Pageable pageable);

    // Returns Slice (without total count)
    Slice<Transaction> findSliceByAccountId(
        String accountId, Pageable pageable);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import com.bank.repository.TransactionRepository;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class PageableService {

    private final TransactionRepository txnRepo;

    public PageableService(TransactionRepository txnRepo) {
        this.txnRepo = txnRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Page<Transaction> getStatementPage(
            String accountId, int page, int size) {
        return txnRepo.findByAccountId(
            accountId,
            PageRequest.of(
                page,
                size,
                Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt")
                    .and(Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "id"))
            )
        );
    }
}
```

```text
PageRequest.of(2, 20, Sort.by(DESC, "createdAt").and(Sort.by(DESC, "id"))):
  page = 2 (third page, 0-indexed)
  size = 20
  sort = ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC

Spring Data generates TWO queries for Page:
  1. Data: SELECT * FROM transactions
           WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
           ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
           LIMIT 20 OFFSET 40
  2. Count: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions
            WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'

Page result:
  page.getContent()       -> List<Transaction> (20 items)
  page.getTotalElements() -> 4000
  page.getTotalPages()    -> 200
  page.getNumber()        -> 2 (current page)
  page.hasNext()          -> true
```

### How is Page different from Slice?

Page runs an extra COUNT query to report total elements and total pages. Slice skips the count and instead fetches one extra row internally to determine whether there is a next page. Slice is cheaper and ideal when you only need "load more" functionality.

| Aspect | Page<T> | Slice<T> |
|---|---|---|
| COUNT query | Yes (extra query) | No |
| Total elements | Available | Not available |
| Total pages | Available | Not available |
| hasNext() | Derived from count | Derived from size+1 fetch |
| Performance | Slower (2 queries) | Faster (1 query) |
| UI pattern | Dashboard with page numbers | Infinite scroll, Load More |
| Large table impact | COUNT can be very slow | No COUNT overhead |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import com.bank.repository.TransactionRepository;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Slice;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class SliceService {

    private final TransactionRepository txnRepo;

    public SliceService(TransactionRepository txnRepo) {
        this.txnRepo = txnRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Slice<Transaction> getMiniStatement(
            String accountId, int page) {
        return txnRepo.findSliceByAccountId(
            accountId,
            PageRequest.of(page, 20,
                Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt"))
        );
    }
    // Only ONE query: LIMIT 21 OFFSET page*20
    // Returns 20 items + knows hasNext from 21st row
    // No COUNT query!
}
```

```text
Slice query:
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC
       LIMIT 21 OFFSET 0  (fetches size+1 = 21 rows)

  If 21 rows returned: hasNext() = true, show 20 to user
  If < 21 rows returned: hasNext() = false, last page

Performance on 10M row table:
  Page: COUNT(*) takes ~500ms + data query ~5ms = ~505ms
  Slice: data query only ~5ms
  100x faster when COUNT is expensive!
```

### How does Sort work?

Spring Data's Sort class composes multi-column ordering. You can chain multiple sort columns with direction, and Spring Data translates them into an ORDER BY clause.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;

public class SortExamples {

    public void demonstrateSort() {
        // Single column, descending
        Sort byDateDesc = Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt");
        // ORDER BY created_at DESC

        // Multi-column with tiebreaker
        Sort byDateAndId = Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt")
            .and(Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "id"));
        // ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC

        // Multiple columns, different directions
        Sort complex = Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "balance")
            .and(Sort.by(Sort.Direction.ASC, "holderName"));
        // ORDER BY balance DESC, holder_name ASC

        // Unsorted (no ORDER BY)
        Sort unsorted = Sort.unsorted();

        // Sort alone (without pagination)
        // repository.findByStatus("ACTIVE", Sort.by("balance").descending());
        // Returns full sorted list without pagination
    }
}
```

```text
Important: always include a unique tiebreaker!

  Sort.by("balance").descending()
  Two accounts with balance=100000: order is non-deterministic!
  Same row may appear on two pages.

  Sort.by("balance").descending().and(Sort.by("id"))
  Deterministic: even with same balance, id breaks the tie.
```

### What are common mistakes with Pageable?

The most common mistakes involve missing tiebreakers, using Page on large tables, and paginating JOIN FETCH queries.

```java
// WRONG: Page on a 50M row table
Page<Transaction> page = repo.findByAccountId(accountId, pageable);
// COUNT(*) on 50M rows: 5-10 seconds per request!
// Every page request pays the full count cost.

// CORRECT: use Slice when total count is not needed
Slice<Transaction> slice = repo.findSliceByAccountId(
    accountId, pageable);
// No COUNT. Fast.
```

```java
// WRONG: paginating a JOIN FETCH query
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.transactions")
Page<Account> findAllWithTransactions(Pageable pageable);
// WARNING: HHH000104: firstResult/maxResults with collection fetch
// Hibernate loads ALL accounts + transactions into memory
// then paginates in-memory. OutOfMemoryError on large tables!

// CORRECT: paginate root entity, then fetch collections
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.status = 'ACTIVE'")
Page<Account> findActiveAccounts(Pageable pageable);
// Paginate accounts first
// Then batch-fetch transactions with @EntityGraph or lazy loading
```

```java
// WRONG: sorting on unindexed column
// Sort.by("holderName") on a table without index on holder_name
// Database performs filesort: sorts entire result set in memory/temp disk
// Slow on large tables!

// CORRECT: add index matching the sort
// CREATE INDEX idx_account_holder ON accounts (holder_name);
// Now Sort.by("holderName") uses the index.
```

### How do you use Pageable in a REST controller?

Spring MVC automatically resolves Pageable from request parameters like page, size, and sort.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import com.bank.repository.TransactionRepository;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.web.PageableDefault;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class StatementController {

    private final TransactionRepository txnRepo;

    public StatementController(TransactionRepository txnRepo) {
        this.txnRepo = txnRepo;
    }

    @GetMapping("/accounts/{id}/transactions")
    public Page<Transaction> getStatement(
            @PathVariable String id,
            @PageableDefault(size = 20, sort = "createdAt",
                direction = org.springframework.data.domain.Sort.Direction.DESC)
            Pageable pageable) {
        return txnRepo.findByAccountId(id, pageable);
    }
}
```

```text
Request: GET /accounts/SAV2001/transactions?page=2&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc

Spring resolves Pageable:
  page = 2
  size = 20
  sort = createdAt DESC

Response includes:
  content: [...20 transactions...]
  totalElements: 4000
  totalPages: 200
  number: 2
  size: 20
  first: false
  last: false
```

### What about List return type vs Page/Slice?

When a repository method returns a List instead of Page or Slice, Spring Data still applies pagination and sorting from the Pageable parameter, but no count query is executed and the result is a plain list.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.List;

public interface TransactionRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Transaction, Long> {

    // Returns List: no count, no hasNext
    List<Transaction> findByAccountId(
        String accountId, Pageable pageable);
    // SQL: LIMIT + OFFSET applied
    // No COUNT query
    // No total info, no hasNext info
    // Cheapest option if you just need the data
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about pagination?

Senior engineers should understand the performance implications of COUNT queries, the interaction between pagination and JOIN FETCH, and when to switch from offset to keyset pagination.

- COUNT(*) on large tables can dominate request time. Use Slice or cache the count.
- JOIN FETCH + Pageable triggers in-memory pagination. Paginate the root entity, then fetch collections.
- For deep scrolling (beyond page 1000), switch to keyset pagination.
- For exports or reports, use streaming (Stream<T>) or ScrollableResults instead of loading pages into memory.
- Always include a unique tiebreaker in Sort to prevent non-deterministic ordering.

